# 8-bit AVR E-Match Firing: Interrupt/Timing Pitfalls & Quick Fixes

## Critical Timing Budget
Typical COTS e-match requires: 15-30V applied for 5-20ms. The "firing window" for reliable ignition is often <10ms total. External interrupts on AVR have inherent latency of 1-2 clock cycles (typically 25-50ns @16MHz) plus ISR execution time - total ISR budget is often 5-10µs minimum.

## Primary Firmware Pitfalls That Let Signals Slip

### 1. ISR Latency Kraken - The "critical section inside ISR" bug
**Pattern seen:**
```c
ISR(INT0_vect) {
    global_disable();  // <-- BAD: blocks system entirely
    if (!arming_ok()) {  // <-- 100-500 CPU cycles wasted
        return;
    }
    fire_match();       // E-match fires = too late
    while(1) { debug(); }  // INFINITE loop - system frozen
}
```
**Pattern people fix:**
```c
ISR(INT0_vect) {
    arming_ready = 1;  // Set atomic flag, no global_disable()
    return;            // ISR exits in <10 cycles
}

// Main loop checks flag:
if (arming_ready) {
    global_disable();
    if (voltage_check(VOCC) > FIRE_THRESH) {  // Hardware-grade threshold
        force_fire_mosfet();
        arming_ready = 0;  // One-shot only
    }
    global_enable();
}
```

### 2. The "Arming Check Everywhere" Race Condition
**Pattern seen:**
```c
void fire_match_unknown() {  // No atomic guard
    if (armed == YES)        // Race! 'armed' changed in ISR
        if (spy_try()        // Another global_disable() inside fire
            send_trigger();  // Missed safe gate window
}
```
**Pattern people fix:**
```c
